00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.880 align:start position:0%
 
welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is cave a cushion hood an associate  professor at the School of Public Health  at yale university professor cush node  is an infectious disease epidemiologist  and his primary research interests are  the epidemiology prevention and control  of hiv/aids and tuberculosis among drug  users prisoners and other at-risk  populations in the United States and in  resource-poor countries he also mentors  researchers from around the world and  teaches courses on hiv/aids global  health and research methods and ethics  today we talk with him about global  health education and how to conduct  research in resource-poor settings  welcome professor Krishna thank you it's  good to be here interest in global  health education has dramatically  increased over the past few years us  particularly here at Yale why do you  think that is it is true i would say in  the last 10 years or so we have seen an  increase in global health interest in  colleges and universities across the  united states i haven't seen a  definitive study a report that talks  about what are the factors that are  behind this interest but there are some  speculations and there are factors that  have been identified and i would say all  these factors are interrelated first I  would say globalization the fact that we  are now connected in ways that we were  not connected before so we can be aware  of events happening halfway around the  world in an instance so if there's a  famine going on or there is there are  health issues we can become aware of  them and the students become very  interested and engaged universities are  becoming globalized the curriculum is  changing there's more international  students that are coming to  and they're more students from Yale  going abroad so they're having these  experiences the other factor i would say  is the AIDS pandemic AIDS is the plague  of our time and I think the devastation  of AIDS pandemic but also the response  has been a source of inspiration and has  captured the imagination of many of our  students so they get very interested in  involved in AIDS related work and then  from there they move on to other global  health issues and I have seen that  happening in our own campus also the  amount of resources that are allocated  to global health has increased  tremendously that is true from public  sources from government but also from  foundations if you just think of the  Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation if you  think of the Clinton Foundation there's  just a lot more funds available which  means there are more jobs and career  choices available for our students we  can't underestimate the role of  celebrities if you think of bottle sure  there are people who are now engaged in  global health that young people tend to  pay attention to and finally i would say  911 and aftermath of 911 and the two  wars that have been going on i think for  some of our students at responds to  what's been happening is a desire to  want to heal the world and they get  involved in global health issues how did  you yourself become involved in global  health well my parents are both  physicians so I was kind of in the  health exposed to health issues from a  young age and also i was born in iran  and came to United States at the age of  17 so I was kind of already familiar  with being an immigrant and some of the  health issues the immigrants have to  deal with so when I chose I was  interested in medicine initially and  then decided to move towards more of a  public health career so I came to Yale  to pursue a public health degree and  when i came in late 80s i was interested  in what was then called international  health and I remember there was  literally a single course at our school  of public health and things have  certainly changed since that and how you  do teach students how to go out into the  world and do research how do you go  about training them what are some things  that in particular that they need to be  very aware of right i would say for  graduate students there are a number of  courses that offers them research  methods and they could be quite equipped  to go into the field to do the work but  I've noticed that among our  undergraduates there weren't too many  courses that prepare them for taking on  independent research projects so a few  years ago i started a global health  research workshop this was a non-credit  workshop which simply engaged with  undergraduate students who increasingly  were asking for assistance to do  independent research in some very  difficult situations so that workshop  went on for about three years and then  in 2009 I turned that into a course so  now there's a course that I teach that I  think you mentioned it it's a global  health research methods and ethics  course and we spend the whole semester  working with undergraduates primarily to  take their ideas an interest in global  health and turn it into a feasible  scientifically sound ethically sound  research projects how can you give an  example of a student that has gone to  Africa for for example and what have  they done there Oh give us a feel for  what exactly they're doing sure the  students actually quite ambitious the  kinds of students that I interact with  tend to want to work in the field they  want to work in resource-poor research  limited countries throughout the world  some of them actually in the United  States I've had students who wanted to  work in inner city or in the  reservations etc and we remind them that  United States is part of the globe so it  is ok to do research on what students  have looked at  past year look that hypertension in  Kenya when we think a lot of the African  countries we often think of infectious  diseases which are still very common and  prevalent there but there's a rise in  chronic diseases and risk factors for  those such as hypertension I had a  student who was quite interested in the  rise in obesity among children in china  so she went there and she worked with  kindergarten students and basically  observed their eating habits and  nutritional habits and sort of did a  study of that are there any surprises or  things that students need to be aware of  that perhaps they they don't necessarily  think of before they embarked on a  project abroad I would say definitely  yes it's always the case that when they  go abroad they are confronted with  situations that they were not really  used to but i must say that increasingly  we're finding that our students are  well-travelled you know they've spent  weeks sometimes months just traveling  with family or just doing volunteer work  but now they want to go back and do an  actual research project nevertheless  they're often surprised by the level of  poverty the witness but also lack of  basic medical care poor infrastructure  talking about roads I'm talking about  electricity I'm talking about having  access to internet they're so used to  being plugged in all the time and  getting answers to questions they may  have and now they're in a situation  where there's no electricity they cannot  connect to the internet and they have to  do a lot of problem-solving on their own  without access to the outside world so  it does tend to surprise them the other  thing that I've heard from students is  the sense of hope and resiliency that  some of the people who are really  struggling with very difficult  situations nevertheless sort of have  this hopefulness about them  and tend to thrive under very difficult  situation and that's quite inspiring for  our students to see what about some of  the ethical considerations do you do  have certain guidelines that students  have to follow around the ethics of  going to another country and dealing  with sensitive perhaps medical  information that kind of thing  absolutely I would say we've been taking  the ethical issues very seriously in the  last couple of years and as I tell my  students ethics and research methods go  hand in hand and ethical issues should  not be an afterthought it should be  something that they think about from the  design phase when they're thinking about  their proposal and beginning to put  their proposal together all the way  through when they're in the field  conducting the research and then coming  back and analyzing the results and  thinking about how those results should  be used to improve the situation that  they may witness so yes there's been a  lot of emphasis on that what are some of  the ethical issue you know what comes up  I would say maybe I think we can put the  ethical issues into three buckets one  are sort of human subjects issues the  kinds of things we think about in terms  of informal consent voluntary  participation etc there are others that  are more about regulation of research so  that includes things like if you want to  go overseas and if you want to do  research in another country the  requirements are that you seek approval  ethical approval from your own  institution so Yale but you also need to  get a local IRB or institutional review  board to approve your project so there  is need for dual approval and some of  the students are not aware of this they  arrive at the sort of host country host  institution and they're surprised to  find out that their Yale approval is not  sufficient so we try to prepare them for  that but I would say that the most  challenging one is personal ethics  because again they're confronted with  issues and situations where they're not  quite sure what to do that they go to do  research and uncover a lot of unmet  health needs whether there are former  child soldiers and they've been  interviewing them about mental health  issues or they're working with street  children in a slums of Kenya they feel  hopeless and helpless in terms of being  able to actually meet the needs of those  children once the research is done so  they come back sometimes quite i would  say disturbed by some of the things they  have seen and they they have a need to  talk about it and we do we've been  increasing the providing sort of forum  for them to discuss these ethical issues  that they have encountered when they  come back and in fact some of the  medical students who came back few years  ago we had a series of discussions and  they decided that these ethical issues  that they have been confronting with  it's worth writing them as a case study  to use as an educational tool and then  we found out there was interest in  medical schools all around the country  in our case studies because they were  being confronted the same issues and  those case studies have now been  published and they have been made  available to other schools where they  are sending students overseas very good  and culturally I would imagine students  must get some kind of training or and  language too so you know how are they  prepared in terms of language and being  culturally aware i would say hugely  important and linguistic skills cultural  competencies I at least tend to  emphasize the fact that if you are going  to another country to conduct research  you must have a working knowledge of the  local language and many students  actually do  it also depends on the kind of research  they're about to undertake if it's  mostly a sort of laboratory based  research project they are not they don't  a certain need to have proficiency in  the local language often the people who  are working in the medical field speak  English but if they're going out into  the community and going into the field  and doing more qualitative research they  really need to understand the language  and they need to be proficient in it  because relying on somebody to translate  for you it's really not the best way to  get valid data sure and finally where do  you think global health education is  going down the road great question the  universities are really this is sort of  what they've been thinking about and  there are literally drafts being  circulated among universities that  begins to define what are the core  competencies so if you're developing a  global health program what are the core  competencies that we want the students  to have and it is an evolving field  people are sort of trying to get their  minds around it and it's very much  something that Yale is involved in and  there's actually a meeting coming up in  November in Montreal and part of that is  to get global health educators together  to talk about some of these issues of  what are the main issues that we need to  communicate to our students and prepare  them very good thank you so much for  being here with us today you must  welcome thank you for more information  about professor cush nut and his  research please visit our website at  yale edu backslash macmillan report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale

